artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider an ultra-dense cell-free massive MIMO (CF-MMIMO) wireless network consisting of a large number L of geographically distributed access points (APs), each equipped with N antennas, jointly serving K single-antenna user equipments (UEs) over the same time-frequency resources. The APs are connected to one or more central processing units via fronthaul links of limited capacity. Let H_l ∈ C^{N×K} denote the (estimated) channel matrix between AP l and the K UEs, and let v_{l,k} ∈ C^N denote the precoding/combining vector used at AP l for UE k.

For benchmark "network-wide" linear processing, one constructs precoders/combiners from the full aggregate channel matrix H = [H_1^T, …, H_L^T]^T ∈ C^{LN×K} using zero-forcing (ZF), V = H(H^H H)^{-1}, or MMSE-style filters; this requires global channel state information and the computation of a pseudo-inverse of size scaling with LN and K, plus high fronthaul overhead.

Because of the distributed topology, the interference power that a given UE receives from different APs (and vice versa) has a large dynamic range: only a small subset of intra-AP interference terms (between co-served UEs at the same AP) and inter-AP interference terms (between different APs) is significant.

Design a family of distributed precoding and combining schemes {v_{l,k}} for CF-MMIMO that simultaneously satisfy:
(i) each AP l only cancels a chosen small subset S_l^{intra} of intra-AP interferers and a small subset S_l^{inter} of inter-AP interferers, identified from large-scale fading and/or instantaneous channel statistics;
(ii) the only pseudo-inverse computations involved at any AP have size bounded by a constant independent of L and much smaller than min(LN, K);
(iii) the additional fronthaul signaling required to construct {v_{l,k}} is bounded independently of L (ideally zero beyond what fully local schemes need);
(iv) as the subsets S_l^{intra}, S_l^{inter} are enlarged, the per-user spectral efficiency provably converges to that of network-wide ZF (or MMSE) processing.

Provide such a scheme together with a rigorous performance analysis (closed-form or tight bounds on the achievable per-user signal-to-interference-plus-noise ratio and spectral efficiency) demonstrating the claimed convergence and complexity/fronthaul scaling, or alternatively prove an impossibility/trade-off result quantifying the minimum complexity and fronthaul required to approach network-wide ZF/MMSE performance within a prescribed gap.

